WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.670
Sailing on a boat headed towards

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.270
StatQuest.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.630
Join me on this boat, let's go to

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.590
StatQuest.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.630
It's super cool.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.550
Hello, and welcome to StatQuest.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.070
StatQuest is brought to you by the

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.670
friendly folks in the genetics

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.710
department at the University of North

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.870
Carolina at Chapel Hill.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.270
Today, we're going to be talking about

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.750
linear regression, aka general linear

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.590
models part one. There's a lot of parts

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.710
to linear models, but it's a really cool

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.110
and powerful concept. So, let's get

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.190
right down to it.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.550
I promise you, I have lots and lots of

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.110
slides that talk about all the

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.990
nitty-gritty details behind linear

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.870
regression. But first, let's talk about

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.350
the main ideas behind it.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.710
The first thing you do in linear

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.670
regression is use least squares to fit a

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.830
line to the data.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.070
The second thing you do is calculate R

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.830
squared.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.510
Lastly, calculate a P value for R

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.229
squared.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:05.830
There are lots of other little things

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.110
that come up along the way, but these

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.470
are the three most important concepts

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.110
behind linear regression.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.590
In the StatQuest fitting a line to data,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.230
we talked about

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.750
fitting a line to data. Duh.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.990
But let's do a quick review.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.350
I'm going to introduce some new

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.390
terminology in this part of the video,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.310
so it's worth watching even if you've

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.070
already seen the earlier StatQuest.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.590
That said, if you need more details,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.270
check that StatQuest out.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.110
For this review, we're going to be

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.350
talking about a data set where we took a

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.270
bunch of mice, and we measured their

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.550
size, and we measured their weight.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.030
Our goal is to use mouse weight as a way

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.910
to predict mouse size.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.710
First, draw a a through the data.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.830
Second, measure the distance from the

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.790
line to the data. Square each distance,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.990
and then add them up.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.030
Terminology alert.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.910
The distance from the line to the data

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.270
point is called a residual.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.310
Third, rotate the line a little bit.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.190
With the new line, measure the

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.590
residuals, square them, and then sum up

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.830
the squares.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.270
Now, rotate the line a little bit more.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.150
Sum up the squared residuals, etc.,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.310
etc., etc. We rotate, and then sum up

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.949
the squared residuals. Rotate, then sum

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:37.630
up the squared residuals. Just keep

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.430
doing that.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.030
After a bunch of rotations, you can plot

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.990
the sum of squared residuals and

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.390
corresponding rotation.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.630
So, in this graph, we have the sum of

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.670
squared residuals on the Y axis, and the

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.230
different rotations on the X axis.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.070
Lastly, you find the rotation that has

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.790
the least sum of squares.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.270
More details about how this is actually

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.710
done in practice are provided in the

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.030
StatQuest on fitting a line to data.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.350
So, we see that this rotation is the one

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.070
with the least squares. So, it will be

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.350
the one to fit to the data.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.990
This is our least squares rotation

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.350
superimposed on the original data.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.590
Bam!

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.750
Now, we know why the method for fitting

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.630
a line is called least squares.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.870
Now, we have fit a line to the data.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.190
This is awesome.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.830
Here's the equation for the line.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.070
Least squares estimated two parameters.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.110
A Y axis intercept

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.110
and a slope.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.030
Since the slope is not zero, it means

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.430
that knowing a mouse's weight will help

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.590
us make a guess about that mouse's size.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.470
How good is that guess?

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.510
Calculating R squared is the first step

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.990
in determining how good that guess will

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.470
be.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.190
The StatQuest R squared explained talks

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.310
about

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.470
you got it R squared.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.310
Let's do a quick review.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.069
I'm also going to introduce some

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.430
additional terminology. So, it's worth

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.430
watching this part of the video even if

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.470
you've seen the original StatQuest on R

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.430
squared.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.510
First, calculate the average mouse size.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.110
Okay, I've just shifted all the data

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.630
points to the Y axis to emphasize that

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.390
at this point we are only interested in

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.670
mouse size.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.310
Here, I've drawn a black line to show

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.230
the average mouse size.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.310
Bam!

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.950
Now, sum the squared residuals.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.750
Just like in least squares, we measure

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.909
the distance from the mean to the data

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:01.750
point and square it and then add those

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.070
squares together.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.590
Terminology alert. We'll call this SS

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.430
mean for sum of squares around the mean.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.550
Note,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.870
the sum of squares around the mean

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.270
equals the data minus the mean squared.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.830
The variation around the mean equals the

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.590
data minus the mean squared divided by

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.350
n.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.670
N is the sample size. In this case, n

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.590
equals 9.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.350
The shorthand notation is the variation

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.510
around the mean equals the sum of

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.670
squares around the mean divided by n,

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.870
the sample size.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.550
Another way to think about variance is

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.750
as the average sum of squares per mouse.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.390
Now, go back to the original plot and

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.030
sum up the squared residuals around our

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.790
least squares fit.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:01.110
We'll call this SS fit for the sum of

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.790
squares around the least squares fit.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.750
The sum of squares around the least

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.670
squares fit is the sum of the distances

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.550
between the data and the line squared.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.990
Just like with the mean, the variance

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.630
around the fit

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.830
is the distance between the line and the

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.990
data squared divided by n, the sample

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.550
size.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.350
The shorthand is the variation around

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.670
the fitted line equals the sum of

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.150
squares around the fitted line divided

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.070
by n, the sample size.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.590
Again, we can think of the variation

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.110
around the fit as the average of the sum

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.350
of squares around the fit for each

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.990
mouse.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.710
In general, the variance of something

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.550
equals the sum of squares divided by the

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.830
number of those things.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.230
In other words, it's an average of sum

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.750
of squares.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.590
I mentioned this because it's going to

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.670
come in handy in a little bit, so keep

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.990
it in the back of your mind.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.710
Okay, let's step back a little bit.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:10.190
This is the raw variation in mouse size.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.350
And this is the variation around the

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.830
least squares line.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.830
There is less variation around the line

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.550
that we fit by least squares. That is to

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.870
say, the residuals are smaller.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.470
As a result, we say that some of the

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.790
variation in mouse size is explained by

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.950
taking mouse weight into account.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.150
In other words, heavier mice are bigger,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.710
lighter mice are smaller.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.150
R squared tells us how much of the

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.710
variation in mouse size can be explained

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.230
by taking mouse weight into account.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.030
This is the formula for R squared. It's

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.550
the variation around the mean minus the

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.350
variation around the fit divided by the

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.310
variation around the mean.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.870
Let's look at an example.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.350
In this example,

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.390
the variation around the mean equals

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.230
11.1

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.870
and the variation around the fit equals

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.670
4.4.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.110
So, we plug those numbers into the

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.590
equation.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.550
The result is that R squared equals 0.6,

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.790
which is the same thing as saying 60%.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.070
This means there is a 60% reduction in

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.110
the variance when we take the mouse

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.630
weight into account.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:32.149
Alternatively,

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:34.909
we can say that mouse weight explains

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:39.310
60% of the variation in mouse size.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.550
We can also use the sum of squares to

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.830
make the same calculation.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.710
This is because when we're talking about

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.670
variation, everything's divided by n,

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.870
the sample size. Since everything's

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.670
scaled by n, we can pull that term out

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.590
and just use the raw sum of squares.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.870
In this case, the sum of squares around

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.470
the mean equals 100

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.590
and the sum of squares around the fit

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.270
equals 40.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.870
Plugging those numbers into the equation

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.870
gives us the same value we had before. R

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.030
squared equals 0.6, which equals 60%.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:19.070
60% of the sums of squares of the mouse

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.190
size can be explained by mouse weight.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.390
Here's another example. We're also going

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:27.310
to go back to using variation in the

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.870
calculation, since that's more common.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.950
In this case, knowing mouse weight means

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.190
you can make a perfect prediction of

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.630
mouse size.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.630
The variation around the mean is the

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.270
same as it was before, 11.1.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.150
But now the variation around the fitted

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.590
line equals zero because there are no

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.310
residuals.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.670
Plugging the numbers in gives us an R

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.390
squared equal to one, which equals 100%.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:01.990
In this case, mouse weight explains 100%

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.710
of the variation in mouse size.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:09.190
Okay. One last example.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.030
In this case, knowing mouse weight

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:15.470
doesn't help us predict mouse size.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.510
If someone tells us they have a heavy

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.710
mouse, well, that mouse could either be

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.430
small or large with equal probability.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:25.870
Similarly, if someone said they had a

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:28.710
light mouse, well, again, we wouldn't

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:30.590
know if it was a big mouse or a small

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.070
mouse because each of those options is

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.070
equally likely.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.190
Just like the other two examples, the

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.270
variation around the mean is equal 11.1.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.790
However, in this case, the variation

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.829
around the fit is also equal 11.1.

00:10:47.839 --> 00:10:50.110
So, we plug those numbers in and we get

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:52.670
R squared equals zero, which equals zero

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.550
percent.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.790
In this case, mouse weight doesn't

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.190
explain any of the variation around the

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.910
mean.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.870
When calculating the sum of squares

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.790
around the mean, we collapse the points

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.590
onto the Y axis just to emphasize the

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.470
fact that we were ignoring mouse weight.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.630
But we could just as easily draw a line

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.829
Y equals the mean mouse size

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:20.950
and calculate the sum of squares around

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:24.150
the mean around that.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:27.430
In this example, we applied R squared to

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.110
a simple equation for a line Y equals

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.670
0.1

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.750
+ 0.78

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.750
* x.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:40.230
This gave us an r-squared of 60% meaning

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.710
60% of the variation in mouse size could

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.670
be explained by mouse weight.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.350
But the concept applies to any equation,

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.950
no matter how complicated.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.910
First, you measure, square, and sum the

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.630
distance from the data to the mean.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.510
Then measure, square, and sum the

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.070
distance from the data to the

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.150
complicated equation.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.670
Once you've got those two sums of

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.830
squares, just plug them in and you've

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.310
got r-squared.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.830
Let's look at a slightly more

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:14.390
complicated example.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.990
Imagine we wanted to know if mouse

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.870
weight and tail length did a good job

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.710
predicting the length of the mouse's

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.230
body.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.510
So we measure a bunch of mice.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.430
To plot this data, we need a

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.310
three-dimensional graph.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.990
We want to know how well weight and tail

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.070
length

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.390
predict body length.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.750
The first mouse we measured had weight =

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.470
2.1,

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.510
tail length = 1.3,

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.310
and body length = 2.5.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:51.590
So that's how we plot this data on this

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.870
3D graph.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.350
Here's all the data in the graph.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.470
The larger circles are points that are

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:01.110
closer to us and represent mice that

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.270
have shorter tails.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.430
The smaller circles are points that are

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.190
further from us and represent mice with

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.670
longer tails.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:13.470
Now we do a least squares fit.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.190
Since we have the extra term in the

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.510
equation representing an extra

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.630
dimension, we fit a plane instead of a

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.350
line.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.670
Here's the equation for the plane.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.670
The y value represents body length.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.350
Least squares estimates three different

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.230
parameters.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:37.150
The first is the Y intercept. That's

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.030
when both tail length and mouse weight

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.350
are equal to zero.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.590
The second parameter, 0.7,

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.070
is for the mouse weight.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.670
The last term,

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.270
0.5,

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.910
is for the tail length.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.150
If we know a mouse's weight and tail

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.070
length, we can use the equation to guess

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.230
the body length.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.870
For example, given the weight and tail

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.390
length for this mouse,

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:09.030
the equation predicts this body length.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:11.310
Just like before, we can measure the

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.950
residuals, square them, and then add

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:17.430
them up to calculate R squared.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:18.510
Now,

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:21.470
if the tail length, or the Z axis, is

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.590
useless and doesn't make the sum of

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.590
squares fit any smaller, then least

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.870
squares will ignore it by making that

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.230
parameter equal to zero.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.630
In this case, plugging the tail length

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:36.110
into the equation would have no effect

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.470
on predicting the mouse size.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.550
This means equations with more

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:44.070
parameters will never make the sum of

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.190
squares around the fit worse than

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.270
equations with fewer parameters.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:50.829
In other words,

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:52.390
this equation,

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.870
mouse size = 0.3

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:59.150
+ mouse weight + flip of a coin +

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:02.430
favorite color + astrological sign +

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:05.390
extra stuff, will never perform worse

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:06.990
than this equation,

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:11.390
mouse size = 0.3 + mouse weight.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.750
This is because least squares will cause

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:15.550
any term that makes sum of squares

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:18.190
around the fit worse to be multiplied by

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:23.110
zero, and in a sense, no longer exist.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.150
Now, due to random chance, there is a

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:29.070
small probability that the small mice in

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:31.630
the data set might get heads more

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:34.630
frequently than large mice.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:36.510
If this happened, then we'd get a

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.870
smaller sum of squares fit

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.830
and a better R squared.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:45.350
Womp womp. Here's the frowny face of sad

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.670
times.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.830
The more silly parameters we add to the

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:52.910
equation, the more opportunities we have

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.110
for random events to reduce sum of

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.670
squares fit and result in a better R

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.150
squared.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.870
Thus, people report an adjusted R

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:05.430
squared value that, in essence, scales R

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:09.030
squared by the number of parameters.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.310
R squared is awesome,

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.750
but it's missing something.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.550
What if all we had were two

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:17.550
measurements?

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.470
We'd calculate the sum of squares around

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.750
the mean. In this case, that would be

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:23.590
10.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:25.390
Then we'd calculate the sum of squares

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:26.829
around the fit,

00:16:26.839 --> 00:16:29.110
which equals zero.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.390
The sum of squares around the fit equals

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.870
zero because you can always draw a

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:37.910
straight line to connect any two points.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.310
What this means is when we calculate R

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.790
squared by plugging the numbers in,

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.910
we're going to get 100%.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:48.950
100% is a great number. We've explained

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.590
all the variation, but any two random

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.630
points will give us the exact same

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:55.710
thing. It doesn't actually mean

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.630
anything.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.990
We need a way to determine if the R

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.150
squared value is statistically

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.110
significant.

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:06.309
We need a P value.

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:09.069
Before we calculate the P value, let's

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:11.350
review the main concepts behind R

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:14.590
squared one last time.

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:17.150
The general equation for R squared is

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.470
the variance around the mean minus the

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:22.230
variance around the fit divided by the

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.429
variance around the mean.

00:17:24.439 --> 00:17:27.270
In our example, this means the variation

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.270
in the mouse size minus the variation

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.150
after taking weight into account divided

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:36.390
by the variation in mouse size.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.950
In other words, r squared equals the

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.750
variation in mouse size explained by

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.510
weight divided by the variation in mouse

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.670
size without taking weight into account.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:51.390
In this particular example, r squared

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.470
equals 0.6,

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.750
meaning we saw a 60% reduction in

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.390
variation once we took mouse weight into

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.190
account.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:01.550
Now that we have a thorough

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.070
understanding of the ideas behind r

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.190
squared, let's talk about the main ideas

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.550
behind calculating a p value for it.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.590
The p value for r squared comes from

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.190
something called f.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:19.070
F is equal to the variation in mouse

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:22.830
size explained by weight divided by the

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.950
variation mouse size not explained by

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:27.670
weight.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:31.030
The numerators for r squared and for f

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:33.190
are the same.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.510
That is to say, it's the reduction in

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:37.830
variance when we take the weight into

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.430
account.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:42.590
The denominator is a little different.

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.590
These dotted lines, the residuals,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.990
represent the variation that remains

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.150
after fitting the line.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:52.230
This is the variation that is not

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:54.430
explained by weight.

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:55.870
So together,

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.870
we have the variation in mouse size

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.910
explained by weight divided by the

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:03.790
variation in mouse size not explained by

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:05.470
weight.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:07.110
Now let's look at the underlying

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.710
mathematics.

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:11.230
Just as a reminder, here's the equation

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.510
for R squared.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:16.190
This is the general equation that will

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:19.590
tell us if R squared is significant.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.150
The meat of these two equations are very

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.830
similar and rely on the same sums of

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:26.670
squares.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:29.270
Like we said before, the numerators are

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.790
the same.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:33.950
In our mouse size and weight example,

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.030
the numerator is the variation in mouse

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:39.110
size explained by weight.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:41.790
And the sum of squares around the fit is

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:44.990
just the residuals squared and summed up

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.990
around the fitted line. So, that's the

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:50.990
variation that the fit does not explain.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.790
These numbers over here are the degrees

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:55.670
of freedom.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.870
They turn the sums of squares into

00:19:57.880 --> 00:19:59.750
variances.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:02.190
I'm going to dedicate a whole StatQuest

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:04.990
to degrees of freedom, but for now,

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.750
let's see if we can get an intuitive

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:10.070
feel for what they're doing here.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.150
Let's start with these.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.190
P fit is the number of parameters in the

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:17.950
fit line.

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:20.270
Here's the equation for the fit line in

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.590
a general format. We just have the Y

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:26.350
intercept plus the slope times X.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:29.390
The Y intercept and the slope are two

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.710
separate parameters.

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:35.190
That means P fit equals two.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:38.310
P mean is the number of parameters in

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.150
the mean line.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.590
In general, that equation is Y equals

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.550
the Y intercept. That's what gives us a

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.550
horizontal line that cuts through the

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:49.830
data.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:52.670
In this case, the Y intercept is the

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.630
mean value.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:58.070
This equation just has one parameter.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.150
Thus, P mean equals one.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.670
Both equations have a parameter for the

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.190
Y intercept.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:10.190
However, the fit line has one extra

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.550
parameter, the slope.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:13.950
In our example,

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:16.230
this slope is the relationship between

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:19.110
weight and size.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:20.670
In this example,

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:25.270
P fit minus P mean equals two minus one,

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.310
which equals one.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:30.870
The fit has one extra parameter, mouse

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.710
weight.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:33.710
Thus,

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:36.630
the numerator is the variance explained

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:38.670
by the extra parameter.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:40.910
In our example, that's the variance in

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:44.910
mouse size explained by mouse weight.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.270
If we had used mouse weight and tail

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:50.870
length to explain variation in size,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.910
then we would end up with an equation

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.670
that had three parameters and P fit

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:57.710
would equal three.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.270
Thus, P fit minus P mean would equal

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:04.830
three minus one, which equals two.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:07.670
Now, the fit has two extra parameters,

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:11.150
mouse weight and tail length.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:14.110
With the fancier equation for the fit,

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.270
the numerator is the variance in mouse

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:19.550
size explained by mouse weight and tail

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.150
length.

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:23.430
Now, let's talk about the denominator

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.710
for our equation for F.

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:28.990
The denominator is the variation in

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:33.590
mouse size not explained by the fit.

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:35.150
That is to say,

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:37.710
it's the sum of squares of the residuals

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.950
that remain after we fit our new line to

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:42.910
the data.

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:46.550
Why divide sum of squares fit by N minus

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.230
P fit instead of just N?

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:51.710
Intuitively,

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:53.230
the more parameters you have in your

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.590
equation,

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:57.430
the more data you need to estimate them.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.070
For example, you only need two points to

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:01.710
estimate a line,

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.910
but you need three points to estimate a

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:05.830
plane.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.790
If the fit is good,

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:09.670
then the variation explained by the

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:11.870
extra parameters in the fit will be a

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.350
large number, and the variation not

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:16.230
explained by the extra parameters in the

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.870
fit will be a small number.

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:22.310
That makes F a really large number.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.750
Now, that question we've all been dying

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:26.390
to know the answer to,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:28.470
how do we turn this number into a P

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:30.310
value?

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.870
Conceptually,

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.790
generate a set of random data.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.790
Calculate the mean and the sum of

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:39.150
squares around the mean.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:41.870
Calculate the fit and the sum of squares

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.070
around the fit.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:46.270
Now, plug all those values into our

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:48.430
equation for F,

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:50.390
and that will give us a number. In this

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.910
case, that number is two.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:56.110
Now, plot that number in a histogram.

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:58.310
Now, generate another set of random

00:23:58.320 --> 00:23:59.550
data.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:01.430
Calculate the mean and the sum of

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.790
squares around the mean.

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:06.110
Then calculate the fit and the sum of

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:08.670
squares around the fit.

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:10.750
Plug those values into our equation for

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:12.350
F,

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.750
and in this case, we get F equals three.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:17.630
So, we then plug that value into our

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:19.550
histogram.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.150
And then we repeat with yet another set

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:23.790
of random data.

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:26.550
In this case, we got F equals one.

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.830
That's plotted on our histogram.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:30.870
And we just keep generating more and

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:33.430
more random data sets, calculating the

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:35.790
sums of squares, plugging them into our

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.350
equation for F, and plotting the results

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:40.270
on our histogram.

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:43.430
Now, imagine we did that hundreds, if

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:45.990
not millions, of times.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.830
When we're all done with our random data

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:50.350
sets, we return to our original data

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:52.070
set.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:54.030
We then plug the numbers into our

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:57.950
equation for F. In this case, we got F =

00:24:57.960 --> 00:24:59.750
6.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.350
The P value is the number of more

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:05.190
extreme values divided by all of the

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:06.670
values.

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.190
So, in this case, we have the value at F

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.990
= 6 and the value at F = 7 divided by

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.270
all the other randomizations that we

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:17.270
created originally.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.070
If this concept is confusing to you, I

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.870
have a StatQuest that explains P values,

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:25.270
so check that one out.

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:27.030
Bam!

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:29.190
You can approximate the histogram with a

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:30.310
line.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:32.990
In practice, rather than generating tons

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.830
of random data sets, people use the line

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:38.470
to calculate the P value.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:41.070
Here's an example of a one standard F

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:43.190
distribution that people use to

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.030
calculate P values.

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.350
The degrees of freedom determine the

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:49.590
shape.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:52.190
The red line represents another standard

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:54.470
F distribution that people use to

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.790
calculate P values.

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:59.590
In this case, the sample size used to

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:01.950
draw the red line is smaller than the

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:05.470
sample size used to draw the blue line.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.510
Notice that when N minus P fit equals

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:13.510
10, the distribution tapers off faster.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:16.070
This means that the P value will be

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:18.310
smaller when there are more samples

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:20.550
relative to the number of parameters in

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:22.710
the fit equation.

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.830
Triple bam!

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:27.790
Hooray! We finally got our P value. Now,

00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:31.070
let's review the main ideas.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.950
Given some data that you think are

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:34.550
related,

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:37.190
linear regression quantifies the

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:39.710
relationship in the data.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.550
This is R squared.

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.990
This needs to be large.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.150
It also determines how reliable that

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:48.150
relationship is.

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:50.350
This is the P value that we calculated

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:51.630
with F.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.710
This needs be small.

